o `processScenario`:\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (\n  scenario: Scenario\n): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const item = scenario.steps[0]?.items[0];\n  const basePremium = item ? BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM : 0;\n  return {\n    results: [{ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE }],\n  };\n};\n```\n\nMass:\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (`scenario`, `item`, `basePremium`) = 3\n- Invocations: 0 = 0 (no function calls)\n- Conditionals: 2 (`?:`, `??`, `?.`) = 12\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 2 (`const item`, `const basePremium`) = 12\nTotal: 28\n\nWait that's higher. Because we added an assignment. Hmm.\n\nWhat if we inline everything:\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (\n  scenario: Scenario\n): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => ({\n  results: [{\n    premium: (scenario.steps[0]?.items[0]\n      ? BASE_PREMIUMS[scenario.steps[0].items[0].type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM\n      : 0) + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  }],\n});\n```\n\nThis removes the assignment and `itemToQuote` binding, but duplicates the property access chain and is much less clear. Mass:\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: 1 (`scenario`) = 1\n- Invocations: 0 = 0\n- Conditionals: 3 (`?:`, `??`, `?.`) = 12\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\nTotal: 14\n\nLower mass but terrible clarity. Rule 2 trumps APP, so don't do this.\n\nI think the type enrichment refactor is the best balance. Let me also check if TypeScript will complain about the `customer` field. The tests pass objects with `customer`, so adding `customer: Customer` to interface is fine - they match.\n\nWait, but in the tests, the scenario objects have `customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 }`. My `Customer` interface has `yearsWithMHPCO: number`. That matches. Good.\n\nAlso, all test scenarios have `steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [...] }]`. My `ScenarioStep` interface has `op: string; items: Item[]`. That matches.\n\nLet me implement this refactor.","thinkingSignature"