omes 66. So I need to update the test expectations.\n\nWait, this is a problem. Many of my early tests for single items expected just base + fee, but actually should include first insurance too. Let me re-check the spec.\n\nThe spec examples show \"base premiums\" before integration. But the integration examples show final premiums including all modifiers. For single-item quotes, there is first insurance surcharge (10%) and processing fee.\n\nFor example, newcomer with cursed sword: final 165. If there were no curse, a single sword for newcomer would be: base 100 + first insurance 10 + fee 5 = 115. But my test 2 expected 105.\n\nSo I need to update all my quote tests to include first insurance. But the test descriptions say \"100 G base + 5 G fee\" etc. Those are wrong if first insurance applies.\n\nActually, looking at the spec examples again: \"The examples below illustrate single rules in isolation. Values shown are base premiums or raw payout amounts before integration with other modifiers, unless stated otherwise.\" So the values shown are base premiums. My tests for single items should expect final premiums including first insurance.\n\nBut wait, that contradicts the test descriptions I wrote. I should update them. However, the TDD workflow says tests come from spec. The spec doesn't explicitly state final premium for a plain single sword. But the rules say first insurance always applies. So the correct final premium for a plain single sword is 115, not 105.\n\nBut my tests 2-7 all expect base+5. This means they'll all fail when I add first insurance. I need to update the test expectations. But the workflow says I should not modify tests once written? Actually, I can fix test expectations if I misinterpreted the spec. The skill says tests come from spec, and if spec clarifies, update.\n\nHmm, but the measurement relies on the test list. Let me think. I should update the active tests to match the correct interpretation. But tests 2-7 are already active and passing. If I change the implementation to add first insurance, they'll fail. I need to update their expectations.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: update all the basic quote test expectations to include first insurance, because that's the correct final premium. But that means changing many already-passing tests at once, which violates \"one test at a time\".\n\nAlternatively, I can keep the implementation minimal and only add first insurance when a test requires it. Test 14 (cursed sword) requires it because expected is 165. If I implement first insurance, tests 2-7 will fail. So I must update them.\n\nLet me update tests 2-7 and 9-13 expectations to include first insurance. But since they're all quote tests, they should all include 10% first insurance. Let me also consider: does first insurance apply to components? Yes, policy-wide.\n\nWait, what about the integration example \"Long-standing customer's second contract\"? It includes first insurance +10 and follow-up -15. So first insurance always applies, follow-up applies to contracts after the first.\n\nWhat determines \"first contract\" vs \"follow-up\"? The spec says \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" And \"This is the customer's second quote in the scenario\". So follow-up applies if this is not the first quote step for that customer. But how do we know? We process steps sequentially. The first quote is first contract; subsequent quotes are follow-up.\n\nSo the `processScenario` function needs to track quote order. For now, with single step scenarios, it's always the first quote, so first insurance applies, no follow-up.\n\nFor test 14, I need first insurance. For tests 2-7, I need to update expected values. Let me do this carefully.\n\nActually, maybe the simplest fix is to update the implementation and tests together. The TDD workflow can handle updating tests if I discover the spec requires it. But I should minimize test changes.\n\nLet me update test 14 implementation