See above for more details.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784923604692},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue: for 3 runes, itemModifiedTotal = 3 × 25 = 75 (because itemModifierFactor = 1 for each rune), policyBase = 60 (block), so total = 75 + 60*0.1 + 5 = 75 + 6 + 5 = 86. But I expected 71 because I thought itemModifiedTotal would be 60.\n\nThe problem is itemModifiedTotal uses per-item base premium (25 each), not the block discounted price. Since components don't have item modifiers, the itemModifiedTotal should equal policyBase for components. But my code computes itemModifiedTotal = 3 × 25 = 75 for 3 runes, while policyBase = 60.\n\nI need to fix this. The itemModifiedTotal should use the same base as policyBase for components, or rather, the block discount should be reflected. \n\nOne approach: compute policyBase as the sum of group premiums with blocks. Then compute itemModifiedTotal by distributing the group base among items of that group, and applying item modifiers. For components without modifiers, this gives exactly the group base.\n\nFor 3 runes: group base = 60, each rune gets 60/3 = 20 as its effective base, itemModifier = 1, so itemModifiedTotal = 60.\nFor 2 runes: group base = 50, each rune = 25, itemModifiedTotal = 50.\nFor cursed sword: group base = 100, sword gets 100, itemModifier = 1.5, itemModifiedTotal = 150.\n\nThis seems right. Let me implement distribute group base among items.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Count items by type.\n- For each type, compute groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count).\n- For each item, effectiveBase = groupBase / count for that type.\n- itemModifiedTotal = sum over items of effectiveBase * itemModifierFactor(item).\n\nBut this changes the meaning for cursed sword: effectiveBase = 100, itemModifier = 1.5, itemModifiedTotal = 150. Good.\n\nFor components in block: effectiveBase = 60/3 = 20, itemModifiedTotal = 60. Good.\n\nFor mixed items: each type's group base is distributed among items of that type.\n\nBut what about \"alike\" components? 2 runes + 1 moonstone: rune group base = 50, moonstone group base = 25. effectiveBase rune = 25, moonstone = 25. itemModifiedTotal = 75. Good.\n\nThis approach works. Let me update quotePremium.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:99","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    itemTypeCounts[item.type] = (itemTypeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  const policyBase = Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce(\n    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n  const itemModifiedTotal = items.reduce(\n    (sum, item) => sum + basePremiumForType(item.type) * itemModifierFactor(item),\n    0\n  );\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + policyBase * (policyWideFactor(customer, quoteIndex) - 1) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};","newText":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer, quoteIndex: number): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    itemTypeCounts[item.type] = (itemTypeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  const policyBase = Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce(\n    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n  const itemModifiedTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemModifierFactor(item);\n  }, 0);\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + policyBase * (policyWideFactor(customer, quoteIndex) - 1) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-