in. Let me be careful.\n\nThe current `calculateQuotePremium` has mixed concerns: counting, base calculation, modifier calculation, customer adjustments, and total. Extracting at least the counting and the adjustments seems beneficial.\n\nLet me calculate mass before and after.\n\nCurrent `calculateQuotePremium` mass:\n```ts\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    itemTypeCounts[item.type] = (itemTypeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  const policyBase = Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce(\n    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n  const itemModifiedTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n  }, 0);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};\n```\n\nConstants: 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 2, 5 (the numeric literals used inline: 0 in reduce, 0 in counts init, 0 in coalesce, 0 in reduce, 0 in coalesce, 2 in loyalty, 5 in fee? Wait PROCESSING_FEE is a binding). Actually constants as literals: 0 (line 3), 0 (line 4 coalesce), 0 (reduce line 8), 0 (line 13 coalesce), 0 (reduce line 16), 0 (reduce line 16), 2 (loyalty), 5 (fee)... but 5 is PROCESSING_FEE binding. Hmm.\n\nLet me be systematic. I'll count only within the function body:\n- `const itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number> = {};` - no literal\n- `itemTypeCounts[item.type] = (itemTypeCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;` - constants: 0, 1 (2)\n- `const policyBase = Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce((sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count), 0);` - constant: 0 (1)\n- `const itemModifiedTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => { ... }, 0);` - constant: 0 (1)\n- `const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);` - no constant\n- `const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];` - no constant\n- `return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);` - no constant\n- `const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;` - constants: 2, 0 (2)\n- `const firstInsuranceSurcharge = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;` - no constant\n- `const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;` - no constant\n- `return roundUp(total);` - no constant\n\nTotal constants: 2 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 6\n\nBindings:\n- items, customer (params) = 2\n- itemTypeCounts = 1\n- item (for loop) = 1\n- policyBase = 1\n- sum, type, count (reduce destructuring) = 3\n- itemModifiedTotal = 1\n- sum, item (reduce) = 2\n- groupBase = 1\n- share = 1\n- loyaltyDiscount = 1\n- firstInsuranceSurcharge = 1\n- total = 1\n\nTotal bindings: 15\n\nInvocations:\n- Object.entries = 1\n- reduce = 2\n- basePremiumForItemGroup = 3 (1 in policyBase, 2 in itemModifiedTotal)\n- itemPremiumMultiplier = 1\n- roundUp = 1\n\nPlus operators: `+` appears multiple times. The APP says \"Invocations (Mass: 2): Function calls (calculate(), Math.max())\". Does `+` count? I think arithmetic operators are not invocations. But actually, maybe binary operators could be considered function calls? The examples given are actual function calls. I'll count only function calls.\n\nTotal invocations: 8\n\nConditionals:\n- ternary `customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? ... : ...` = 1\n- `??` coalescing: is this a conditional? The guidelines say \"Conditional (Mass: 4): Control flow (if, switch, ?: )\". `??` is a nullish coalescing operator, similar to a conditional. I'll count the two `??` as conditionals = 2.\n\nTotal conditionals: 3\n\nLoops:\n- `for...of` = 1\n- `reduce` loops = 2\n\nTotal loops: 3\n\nAssignments:\n- `itemTypeCounts[item.type] = ...` = 1\n\nTotal assignments: 1\n\nTotal mass = 6*1 + 15*1 + 8*2 + 3*4 + 3*5 + 1*6 = 6 + 15 + 16 + 12 + 15 + 6 = 70\n\nHmm, that's high. But the APP mass is supposed to measure complexity. Let me think if this is right. Actually, maybe `??` shouldn't count as conditionals. The guidelines specifically list `if, switch, ?:`. I'll be conservative and not count `??` as conditionals, but note it.\n\nWithout `??`: conditionals = 1. Total = 6 + 15 + 16 + 4 + 15 + 6 = 62.\n\nStill high. The high mass comes from the function being complex.\n\nNow, let me think about a refactoring that reduces mass and improves intent.\n\nRefactoring idea: Extract helpers to clarify the quote calculation.\n\n```ts\nconst countItemsByType = (items: Item[]): Record<string, number> => {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts[item.type] = (counts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst policyBaseFor = (itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce(\n    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n\nconst itemModifiedTotalFor = (\n  items: Item[],\n  itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>\n): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n  }, 0);\n\nconst customerAdjustmentsFor = (\n  policyBase: number,\n  customer: Customer\n): { firstInsuranceSurcharge: number; loyaltyDiscount: number } => ({\n  firstInsuranceSurcharge: policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE,\n  loyaltyDiscount:\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0,\n});\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = policyBaseFor(itemTypeCounts);\n  const itemModifiedTotal = itemModifiedTotalFor(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const { firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount } = customerAdjustmentsFor(policyBase, customer);\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};\n```\n\nThis adds several functions but makes the main function much clearer.\n\nMass calculation for refactored `calculateQuotePremium` only:\nConstants: 0\nBindings: items, customer, itemTypeCounts, policyBase, itemModifiedTotal, firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount = 7\nInvocations: countItemsByType, policyBaseFor, itemModifiedTotalFor, customerAdjustmentsFor, roundUp = 5\nConditionals: 0\nLoops: 0\nAssignments: 0\n\nMass = 0 + 7 + 10 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 17\n\nPlus new helper functions:\n- `countItemsByType`: constants 0,1 = 2; bindings items, counts, item = 3; invocations = 0; conditionals = 1 (??); loops = 1 (for); assignments = 1. Mass = 2+3+0+4+5+6 = 20. (Without counting ??: 16)\n- `policyBaseFor`: constants 0 = 1; bindings itemTypeCounts, sum, type, count = 4; invocations Object.entries, reduce, basePremiumForItemGroup = 3; conditionals 0; loops 1 (reduce); assignments 0. Mass = 1+4+6+0+5+0 = 16.\n- `itemModifiedTotalFor`: constants 0 = 1; bindings items, itemTypeCounts, sum, item, groupBase, share = 6; invocations reduce, basePremiumForItemGroup, itemPremiumMultiplier = 3; conditionals 0; loops 1 (reduce); assignments 0. Mass = 1+6+6+0+5+0 = 18.\n- `customerAdjustmentsFor`: constants 0, 2 = 2; bindings policyBase, customer = 2; invocations = 0; conditionals 1 (ternary); loops 0; assignments 0. Mass = 2+2+0+4+0+0 = 8.\n\nTotal new helper mass = 20 + 16 + 18 + 8 = 62 (with ??) or 58 (without ??).\n\nOriginal `calculateQuotePremium` mass = 70 (with ??) or 62 (without ??).\n\nSo adding helpers actually doesn't reduce mass much and adds elements (Rule 4 concern). However, the clarity improvement is significant.\n\nHmm, maybe I should look for a simpler refactoring.\n\nAnother idea: The `itemModifiedTotal` calculation is complex. Maybe we can simplify it.\n\nActually, wait. There's a subtle issue: for a group of items, the current code calculates:\n- `policyBase` = sum of group bases\n- `itemModifiedTotal` = sum over items of (groupBase/count * itemMultiplier)\n\nThis means the modified total can differ from policyBase only when items have different multipliers. If all items in a group have no modifiers, itemModifiedTotal equals groupBase. If some have modifiers, the modified total differs.\n\nThis is actually a reasonable design. But the implementation could be clearer.\n\nAlternative simplification: Instead of computing per-item share from group base, compute the total as:\n```\npolicyBase + sum over items of (share * (multiplier - 1))\n```\n\nBut this is equivalent and may not be clearer.\n\nAnother idea: The `roundUp` function with `Math.ceil(value - 0.0001)` is specific. The `- 0.0001` is to handle floating point edge cases. We could rename it to `roundUpInFavorOfInsurer` or add a comment.\n\nLet me think about a focused refactoring that improves Rule 2 with minimal mass increase.\n\nI think the best simple refactor is to extract `countItemsByType` and add a helper for the final premium assembly. Actually, maybe just extracting `countItemsByType` is a good minimal step.\n\nBut the instructions say \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring\" and \"Make ONE improvement at a time\". Let me do one focused improvement: extract helper functions to reveal the steps in quote premium calculation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The current code is already reasonably well-structured with named constants and helper functions. The main issue is that `calculateQuotePremium` does too much. Extracting helpers will improve readability.\n\nBut I want to be careful about mass. Let me think about whether there's duplication that can be removed.\n\nI see that `basePremiumForItemGroup` is called with `item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]` multiple times. If I extract a helper like `basePremiumFor(item, itemTypeCounts)`, I could reduce duplication:\n\n```ts\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item, itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n```\n\nBut this only saves one invocation per use and adds a function. Not worth it.\n\nAnother observation: The `itemModifiedTotal` calculation duplicates the logic of getting the count and group base for each item. We could precompute a map of type to per-item share:\n\n```ts\nconst perItemShareForType = (type: string, count: number): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count) / count;\n```\n\nHmm, but this might be overkill.\n\nLet me think about what is the cleanest, most intent-revealing refactor.\n\nI think extracting the following helpers makes sense:\n1. `countItemsByType` - obvious intent improvement\n2. `customerAdjustmentsFor` or just extract the discount/surcharge logic\n\nLet me also look at `processScenario`:\n```ts\nexport const processScenario = (\n  scenario: Scenario\n): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const itemsToQuote = scenario.steps[0]?.items ?? [];\n  return {\n    results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(itemsToQuote, scenario.customer) }],\n  };\n};\n```\n\nIt only handles quote operation (step[0]). There are `op` field and likely future operations like claim. But tests only cover quote. This is fine for now.\n\nThe `itemsToQuote` name is good. Maybe `firstStepItems` would be more accurate since it takes `steps[0]`, but `itemsToQuote` reveals intent for current scope.\n\nOK, let me make a decision. I'll do these refactorings:\n\n1. **Extract `countItemsByType`** - Improves intent of counting logic.\n2. **Extract `customerAdjustmentsFor`** - Bundles related surcharge/discount logic and reveals intent.\n3. **Rename `roundUp` to `roundUpForMHPCO`** or keep with comment? Actually, I'll keep the name but add a comment explaining the epsilon. Or maybe rename to `roundUpWithEpsilon`. Hmm, `roundUp` is clear enough; the implementation detail (epsilon) can be explained in a comment.\n\nWait, let me re-read the instructions: \"Naming is first priority\". I should evaluate names carefully.\n\n`roundUp` - name says it rounds up. But the implementation `Math.ceil(value - 0.0001)` is not standard rounding. Actually, it IS rounding up, just with a tiny epsilon to handle floating point issues. The name is fine.\n\n`calculateQuotePremium` - good.\n\n`basePremiumForItemGroup` - The function returns the base premium for a group of same-type items, considering component blocks. Maybe `groupBasePremium` would be slightly more concise but `basePremiumForItemGroup` is clear.\n\n`itemPremiumMultiplier` - returns the multiplier. Good.\n\n`processScenario` - processes a scenario. Good.\n\nActually, I realize there's a potential name issue: `basePremiumForItemGroup` doesn't reveal that it's specifically about same-type item groups and component blocks. But the existing name is adequate.\n\nLet me consider a more substantial refactor: reorganize the code to have a clear pipeline:\n\n```ts\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = calculatePolicyBase(itemTypeCounts);\n  const itemModifiedTotal = calculateItemModifiedTotal(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = calculateFirstInsuranceSurcharge(policyBase);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = calculateLoyaltyDiscount(policyBase, customer);\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};\n```\n\nThis is very clear. Each helper has one responsibility.\n\nMass for main function: bindings 7, invocations 5, total = 17.\nHelpers:\n- `countItemsByType`: ~16-20\n- `calculatePolicyBase`: ~16\n- `calculateItemModifiedTotal`: ~18\n- `calculateFirstInsuranceSurcharge`: mass = bindings 2 (policyBase, ... wait it's just one param), constants 0? Actually no constants. mass = 1 (binding) + 1 (invocation for multiplication? No, * is not invocation). So mass = 1. But this is overkill - too few elements.\n- `calculateLoyaltyDiscount`: constants 2, 0; binding policyBase, customer; conditional 1. mass = 2+2+4 = 8.\n\nHmm, extracting tiny helpers like `calculateFirstInsuranceSurcharge` adds elements without much benefit.\n\nLet me instead extract:\n1. `countItemsByType`\n2. `policyBaseFor` \n3. `itemModifiedTotalFor`\n4. Keep surcharge/discount inline but with better structure\n\nActually, I think extracting `countItemsByType`, `policyBaseFor`, and `itemModifiedTotalFor` is reasonable. These are meaningful operations.\n\nBut adding 3 helpers might be a lot. Let me check the mass tradeoff.\n\nIf I extract just `countItemsByType`:\n- Original calculateQuotePremium mass (without counting the extracted part): The for loop part has constants 0,1; bindings itemTypeCounts,item; conditionals 1 (??); loops 1; assignments 1. mass = 2+2+0+4+5+6 = 19. (Wait invocations in that part = 0)\n- New calculateQuotePremium without for loop: loses those 19 but gains invocation countItemsByType (2) and binding itemTypeCounts (1). So new main mass = 70 - 19 + 2 + 1 = 54.\n- New helper countItemsByType mass = 16 (without ??) or 20 (with ??).\n- Total mass = 54 + 16 = 70 (without ??) or 74 (with ??).\n\nSo extracting just counting doesn't reduce mass (if we count helpers) but improves clarity.\n\nIf I extract all three:\n- New main mass ≈ 17\n- Helpers mass ≈ 16 + 16 + 18 = 50\n- Total ≈ 67\n\nSo mass is similar but clarity is much better.\n\nMass is a heuristic; Rule 2 (clarity) trumps mass. So this is acceptable.\n\nBut wait, I'm counting `??` as conditional. If I don't count it, original mass is 62, refactored is about 63. Similar.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what counts as what. The APP is a simple heuristic. I shouldn't overthink it. I'll provide reasonable counts and note any assumptions.\n\nLet me now implement the refactoring. I'll extract three helpers and restructure `calculateQuotePremium`.\n\nBefore that, let me also consider if there's a way to reduce actual duplication. Looking at `basePremiumForItemGroup`, it's called with the same args multiple times. But the args come from different contexts, so no direct duplication.\n\nOne more thing: the `itemModifiedTotal` uses `basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type])` and divides by `itemTypeCounts[item.type]`. This is essentially computing the per-item base premium. We could extract:\n\n```ts\nconst perItemBasePremium = (item: Item, itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]) / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n```\n\nThis would make the code more readable:\n```ts\nconst itemModifiedTotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n  return sum + perItemBasePremium(item, itemTypeCounts) * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n}, 0);\n```\n\nBut this adds another helper. Let me see if it reduces mass in `itemModifiedTotalFor`.\n\nCurrent `itemModifiedTotalFor` (extracted version):\n```ts\nconst itemModifiedTotalFor = (items: Item[], itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n  }, 0);\n```\n\nMass: constants 0; bindings items, itemTypeCounts, sum, item, groupBase, share = 6; invocations reduce, basePremiumForItemGroup, itemPremiumMultiplier = 3; conditionals 0; loops 1 (reduce); assignments 0. Mass = 1+6+6+0+5+0 = 18.\n\nWith `perItemBasePremium`:\n```ts\nconst perItemBasePremium = (item: Item, itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]) / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n\nconst itemModifiedTotalFor = (items: Item[], itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + perItemBasePremium(item, itemTypeCounts) * itemPremiumMultiplier(item), 0);\n```\n\nMass for `perItemBasePremium`: bindings item, itemTypeCounts = 2; invocations basePremiumForItemGroup = 1; mass = 2 + 2 = 4.\nMass for new `itemModifiedTotalFor`: constants 0; bindings items, itemTypeCounts, sum, item = 4; invocations reduce, perItemBasePremium, itemPremiumMultiplier = 3; loops 1; mass = 1 + 4 + 6 + 0 + 5 + 0 = 16.\n\nTotal = 4 + 16 = 20, slightly higher than 18. But it removes the `share` explaining variable and `groupBase` variable, which might reduce clarity (share is a useful name). So probably not worth it.\n\nLet me keep `share` as an explaining variable - it's good for Rule 2.\n\nOK, I'll implement these refactorings:\n1. Extract `countItemsByType`\n2. Extract `policyBaseFor`\n3. Extract `itemModifiedTotalFor`\n4. Extract `customerAdjustmentsFor` (maybe not - keep inline?)\n\nActually, I think extracting `customerAdjustmentsFor` returning an object is a bit clunky. Let me keep surcharge/discount inline but compute them in a clearer way. Or extract two simple helpers:\n- `firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(policyBase)` \n- `loyaltyDiscountFor(policyBase, customer)`\n\nThese are tiny but very clear. They remove the local variable assignments in the main function.\n\nHmm, but each is one-line. Is that worth it? It reduces mass in main function and makes intent very clear. Let me do it.\n\nActually, let me not over-engineer. I'll extract:\n1. `countItemsByType`\n2. `policyBaseFor`\n3. `itemModifiedTotalFor`\n4. `firstInsuranceSurchargeFor`\n5. `loyaltyDiscountFor`\n\nThis makes `calculateQuotePremium` extremely clear:\n\n```ts\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = policyBaseFor(itemTypeCounts);\n  const itemModifiedTotal = itemModifiedTotalFor(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(policyBase);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = loyaltyDiscountFor(policyBase, customer);\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};\n```\n\nEach helper has a single, clear responsibility.\n\nBut this is 5 new helpers. Is that too many elements? Rule 4 says fewest elements. But these elements serve clear purpose (Rule 2).\n\nI think a better balance is to extract just 3 helpers:\n1. `countItemsByType`\n2. `policyBaseFor`\n3. `itemModifiedTotalFor`\n\nAnd keep the surcharge/discount inline. The main function will be:\n\n```ts\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = policyBaseFor(itemTypeCounts);\n  const itemModifiedTotal = itemModifiedTotalFor(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const total = itemModifiedTotal + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return roundUp(total);\n};\n```\n\nThis is clear. The inline surcharge/discount are simple enough.\n\nLet me also consider extracting `customerAdjustmentsFor` to return both:\n\n```ts\nconst customerAdjustmentsFor = (policyBase: number, customer: Customer) => ({\n  firstInsuranceSurcharge: policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE,\n  loyaltyDiscount: customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0,\n});\n```\n\nThen main function:\n```ts\nconst { firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount } = customerAdjustmentsFor(policyBase, customer);\n```\n\nThis groups related logic but introduces object destructuring. Mass is similar.\n\nI think I'll go with the 3-helper approach. It's clean and not over-engineered.\n\nWait, I should also think about `basePremiumForItemGroup` - is its name the best? It handles both regular groups and component blocks. Maybe `basePremiumForSameTypeItems` is clearer. But renaming would require updating references. The name `basePremiumForItemGroup` is okay.\n\nAnother thing: `COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES` is a Set. Using a Set is fine but maybe `BLOCK_TYPES` is shorter. The name is clear enough.\n\nLet me also consider the order of functions. Currently constants are at top, then interfaces, then helpers, then main functions. This is a reasonable order.\n\nOK, I'll implement the refactor. Let me write the new `claim-office.ts`.\n\nActually, I want to be careful. Let me make the edit.\n\nOriginal code structure:\n```ts\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { ... };\n\nconst DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\n\ninterface Customer { ... }\ninterface Item { ... }\ninterface ScenarioStep { ... }\ninterface Scenario { ... }\n\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM;\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst basePremiumForItemGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => { ... };\n\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\n\nconst itemPremiumMultiplier = (item: Item): number => ...;\n\nconst roundUp = (value: number): number => Math.ceil(value - 0.0001);\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => { ... };\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): ... => { ... };\n```\n\nNew structure - I'll insert helpers before `calculateQuotePremium` and after `itemPremiumMultiplier`:\n\n```ts\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { ... };\n\nconst DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\n\ninterface Customer { ... }\ninterface Item { ... }\ninterface ScenarioStep { ... }\ninterface Scenario { ... }\n\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM;\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst basePremiumForItemGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => { ... };\n\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\n\nconst itemPremiumMultiplier = (item: Item): number => ...;\n\nconst roundUp = (value: number): number => Math.ceil(value - 0.0001);\n\nconst countItemsByType = (items: Item[]): Record<string, number> => { ... };\n\nconst policyBaseFor = (itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number => ...;\n\nconst itemModifiedTotalFor = (items: Item[], itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number => ...;\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[], customer: Customer): number => { ... };\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): ... => { ... };\n```\n\nGood. Let me write the file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.write:3","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","content":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\n\ninterface Customer {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n}\n\ninterface Item {\n  type: string;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  material?: string;\n}\n\ninterface ScenarioStep {\n  op: string;\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface Scenario {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: ScenarioStep[];\n}\n\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_