tring, itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemGroup(type, itemTypeCounts[type]) / itemTypeCounts[type];\n```\n\nThen:\n```\nconst totalModifiedItemPremiumFor = (items, itemTypeCounts) =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + shareOfGroupBaseFor(item.type, itemTypeCounts) * itemPremiumMultiplier(item), 0);\n```\n\nThis adds a function and param binding, removes 2 locals. Mass probably similar or slightly higher. Clarity? It names the concept of \"share of group base\" which is good. But it might be overkill. The current inline calculation is clear enough with `groupBase` and `share` variable names.\n\nI think the current refactoring is sufficient. Let me finalize.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the current `calculateQuotePremium`:\n\n```\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number\n): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = policyBaseFor(itemTypeCounts);\n  const totalModifiedItemPremium = totalModifiedItemPremiumFor(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const modifiers = quoteModifiersFor(policyBase, customer, quoteIndex);\n  return roundUp(totalModifiedItemPremium + modifiers + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n```\n\nThis reads well. The flow is:\n1. Count items\n2. Compute policy base\n3. Compute modified item premium total\n4. Compute quote modifiers\n5. Round up total\n\nGood.\n\nNow I need to remove the backup file and provide the final report. Actually, I should keep the backup? No, remove it. It's not needed and would be clutter.\n\nWait, should I run tests one final time after removing backup? The backup doesn't affect tests. But I'll remove it and run tests to confirm.\n\nActually, let me also check if there's a linter or type checker I should run. The project uses TypeScript. Let me run `pnpm test` already passed. Maybe `tsc --noEmit`? But tests compile TS via vitest. I think we're fine.\n\nLet me remove the backup and run tests one more time.","th