mium` by `count` which gives `basePremium`. The purpose is to distribute group base premium equally and then apply per-item multipliers. This is clear enough.\n\nLet me look at potential duplication. The `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` maps are conceptually similar - both map item type to a value. But they serve different purposes and have different values. Combining them might reduce elements but hurt clarity.\n\nAnother duplication: the enchantment check `>= 5` appears only once. Cursed check once. Loyalty check once.\n\nI think the best refactoring is naming improvements plus extracting the claim handling. But I want to keep mass reasonable.\n\nLet me consider a more impactful refactoring: merging `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` into an `ITEM_DATA` record. This would reduce constants (fewer object wrappers) and bindings. But it might hurt clarity because the two values have different semantics. Also, DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM and DEFAULT_INSURANCE_VALUE differ.\n\nHmm. Merging them:\n```ts\nconst ITEM_DATA: Record<string, { basePremium: number; insuranceValue: number }> = {\n  sword: { basePremium: 100, insuranceValue: 1000 },\n  ...\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces:\n- 2 constants maps + 2 defaults → 1 map + 2 functions\nBut adds object literals per item, so constants don't reduce much.\n\nActually it might increase mass due to more object property access. Not worth it.\n\nWhat about extracting the \"per-item\" logic? Currently `itemPremiumMultiplier` handles cursed and enchantment. We could extract each condition, but that adds functions.\n\nI think the cleanest refactorings are:\n1. Rename `policyBaseFor` to `totalBasePremiumFor`\n2. Rename `insuranceSumFor` to `totalInsuranceValueFor`\n3. Extract claim step handling to `processClaimStep`\n4. Add `DEDUCTIBLE` constant\n\nLet me do these and recalculate mass. But I want to ensure mass doesn't go up too much.\n\nActually, for the claim extraction, I need to be careful about the `policies` array mutation. The function can take policies and step, return payout and update cap.\n\nLet me design `processClaimStep`:\n```ts\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\nconst processClaimStep = (\n  policies: Array<{ items: Item[]; cap: number }>,\n  step: ScenarioStep\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const policy = policies[step.policy ?? 0];\n  const damageAmount = step.incident?.damages[0]?.amount ?? 0;\n  const grossPayout = Math.min(damageAmount, policy.cap) - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  const payout = Math.max(0, grossPayout);\n  policy.cap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.cap };\n};\n```\n\nThis adds:\n- 1 constant (DEDUCTIBLE = 100) - replaces the 100 literal, so net 0 change in constants\n- 1 binding (DEDUCTIBLE)\n- Function: processClaimStep with params policies, step → 2 bindings\n- damageAmount, grossPayout, payout → 3 bindings, 3 assignments\n- 1 invocation (Math.min), 1 invocation (Math.max)\n- 1 conditional (? in step.policy ?? 0), 1 conditional (? in damages[0]?.amount ?? 0)\n- 1 assignment (policy.cap -= payout)\n\nIn the main function, we remove:\n- policy, payout, finalPayout bindings (3)\n- Math.min, Math.max invocations (2)\n- ?? invocations (2)\n- 100 constant\n- policy.cap -= finalPayout assignment (1)\n- conditional logic stays same\n\nWe add:\n- processClaimStep invocation (1)\n- step binding still there\n\nHmm, this might slightly increase mass due to function overhead. But it improves clarity significantly by giving the claim step its own named function.\n\nActually, looking at the rules, Rule 2 (reveals intent) trumps APP. So clarity is more important than mass. But I should also look for mass reductions.\n\nAnother idea: use a single `capMultiplier` constant for `* 2` in policy cap. Adds a binding but replaces literal.\n\nWhat about the `basePremiumForItemGroup` function? It has special handling for component blocks. Could we simplify? The rule is: for runes/moonstones, exactly 3 form a block costing 60. Otherwise, count * base. This is clear.\n\nActually, I think the biggest clarity improvement is extracting the claim step. But I also want to reduce mass. Let me see if there's a way to simplify `totalModifiedItemPremiumFor`.\n\nWait, the function `totalModifiedItemPremiumFor` distributes the group base equally among items of the same type and then multiplies by item-specific multipliers. This is correct for the current tests. The division and multiplication could be simplified conceptually: for each item, its \"share\" of the group base, times multiplier. It's fine.\n\nLet me think about Rule 4 (fewest elements). Are there unnecessary abstractions? The constants are all meaningful. The functions all serve clear purposes. No unnecessary elements.\n\nOK, let me do the refactoring with these changes:\n1. Rename `policyBaseFor` → `totalBasePremiumFor`\n2. Rename `insuranceSumFor` → `totalInsuranceValueFor`\n3. Add `DEDUCTIBLE = 100` constant\n4. Extract `processClaimStep` function\n\nActually, for mass reduction, maybe I can inline `quoteModifiersFor` into `calculateQuotePremium`? That would reduce function overhead (bindings, invocations). But it would hurt clarity - the modifiers are a distinct concept. Rule 2 says clarity trumps. Keep separate.\n\nWhat about combining `basePremiumForType` and `insuranceValueForType`? They both do `MAP[type] ?? DEFAULT`. That's conceptual duplication. But extracting a generic `lookupValue` function might hurt clarity.\n\nActually, I could extract a helper:\n```ts\nconst valueForType = <T>(map: Record<string, T>, type: string, defaultValue: T): T =>\n  map[type] ?? defaultValue;\n```\n\nThen:\n```ts\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number =>\n  valueForType(BASE_PREMIUMS, type, DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM);\n\nconst insuranceValueForType = (type: string): number =>\n  valueForType(INSURANCE_VALUES, type, DEFAULT_INSURANCE_VALUE);\n```\n\nThis removes duplication. Mass impact:\n- Add function `valueForType` with 3 params, 1 conditional (??), 1 binding (map), 1 binding (type), 1 binding (defaultValue)\n- Add 2 invocations of valueForType\n- Remove 2 ?? invocations\n- Net: +1 function (3 bindings + 1 conditional), +2 invocations, -2 ?? invocations, -2 bindings (type in each)\n\nActually this is interesting. It reduces conceptual duplication. Let me consider it.\n\nHmm, but `valueForType` is generic and might be overkill. Rule 4 says fewest elements. Is it worth it? It removes duplicated `?? DEFAULT` pattern. I think it's reasonable but maybe not necessary. It adds abstraction.\n\nI'll stick with the clearer refactorings that don't over-abstract.\n\nLet me reconsider. The mandatory requirement is to attempt at least one refactoring. I can do naming improvements alone. But let me do a few clean ones.\n\nPlan:\n1. Rename `policyBaseFor` → `totalBasePremiumFor`\n2. Rename `insuranceSumFor` → `totalInsuranceValueFor`\n3. Extract `processClaimStep` function\n4. Add `DEDUCTIBLE = 100` constant\n\nLet me implement these.\n\nActually, I realize `insuranceSumFor` is used only once in the code, but the name `insuranceSumFor(items)` is okay. `totalInsuranceValueFor(items)` is more explicit. I'll rename.\n\nFor the claim function, I need to make sure types are correct. The step has optional policy and incident, but in claim steps they're expected.\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```ts\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM = 100;\n\n// ... interfaces ...\n\nconst basePremiumForType = (type: string): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS[type] ?? DEFAULT_BASE_PREMIUM;\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst basePremiumForItemGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.has(type) && count === BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumForType(type);\n};\n\nconst CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\n\nconst itemPremiumMultiplier = (item: Item): number =>\n  1 +\n  (item.cursed ? CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0) +\n  ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 5 ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0);\n\nconst roundUp = (value: number): number => Math.ceil(value - 0.0001);\n\nconst countItemsByType = (items: Item[]): Record<string, number> => {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts[item.type] = (counts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\nconst DEFAULT_INSURANCE_VALUE = 0;\n\nconst insuranceValueForType = (type: string): number =>\n  INSURANCE_VALUES[type] ?? DEFAULT_INSURANCE_VALUE;\n\nconst totalInsuranceValueFor = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForType(item.type), 0);\n\nconst totalBasePremiumFor = (itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>): number =>\n  Object.entries(itemTypeCounts).reduce(\n    (sum, [type, count]) => sum + basePremiumForItemGroup(type, count),\n    0\n  );\n\nconst totalModifiedItemPremiumFor = (\n  items: Item[],\n  itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>\n): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n  }, 0);\n\nconst quoteModifiersFor = (\n  policyBase: number,\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number\n): number => {\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 ? policyBase * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount =\n    quoteIndex > 0 ? policyBase * FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  return firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  quoteIndex: number\n): number => {\n  const itemTypeCounts = countItemsByType(items);\n  const policyBase = totalBasePremiumFor(itemTypeCounts);\n  const totalModifiedItemPremium = totalModifiedItemPremiumFor(items, itemTypeCounts);\n  const modifiers = quoteModifiersFor(policyBase, customer, quoteIndex);\n  return roundUp(totalModifiedItemPremium + modifiers + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\n\nconst processClaimStep = (\n  policies: Array<{ items: Item[]; cap: number }>,\n  step: ScenarioStep\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const policy = policies[step.policy ?? 0];\n  const damageAmount = step.incident?.damages[0]?.amount ?? 0;\n  const grossPayout = Math.min(damageAmount, policy.cap) - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  const payout = Math.max(0, grossPayout);\n  policy.cap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.cap };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (\n  scenario: Scenario\n): { results: Array<{ premium?: number; payout?: number; remainingCap?: number }> } => {\n  const policies: Array<{ items: Item[]; cap: number }> = [];\n  const results: Array<{ premium?: number; payout?: number; remainingCap?: number }> = [];\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({\n        premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, results.length),\n      });\n      policies.push({ items: step.items, cap: totalInsuranceValueFor(step.items) * 2 });\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const { payout, remainingCap } = processClaimStep(policies, step);\n      results.push({ payout, remainingCap });\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nWait, `processClaimStep` mutates `policy.cap`