omething clearer.\n\nOption C: Extract `applyClaimToDamage` helper.\n\nLet me think about what has the most value. The recent green phase was \"Loop over all damages in a claim, applying per-damage deductible and cap.\" So the claim calculation logic is fresh.\n\nI think the most valuable refactoring is to make `calculateClaimPayout` pure and self-contained by returning both payout and remainingCap, eliminating the need for `processScenario` to separately track and mutate `policy.cap`. This improves intent (Rule 2) and reduces duplication (Rule 3).\n\nBut wait - we must keep tests green. Let me make sure the logic is equivalent.\n\nCurrently:\n- `policy.cap` starts as total insurance value * 2\n- `calculateClaimPayout(policy.cap, step, policy.items)` returns totalPayout\n- `policy.cap -= payout` sets it to `policy.cap - totalPayout`\n- The final remainingCap inside calculateClaimPayout is also `policyCap - totalPayout`\n\nSo if calculateClaimPayout returns `{ payout, remainingCap }`, we can do:\n```\nconst { payout, remainingCap } = calculateClaimPayout(policy.cap, step, policy.items);\nresults.push({ payout, remainingCap });\n```\nAnd remove `policy.cap -= payout;`. But then policies array is no longer needed to track cap? Actually we still need policies array to look up items for future claims. But we don't need to mutate cap if we pass the current cap each time.\n\nWait, but if there are multiple claims on the same policy, we need the remaining cap from the previous claim. Currently policy.cap is mutated. If calculateClaimPayout returns remainingCap, we'd need to store it somewhere. We could store it back in policies array, or compute it differently.\n\nActually, we could keep `policy.cap` but assign the returned remainingCap to it. That's still mutation but more explicit:\n```\nconst claimResult = calculateClaimPayout(policy.cap, step, policy.items);\npolicy.cap = claimResult.remainingCap;\nresults.push(claimResult);\n```\n\nHmm, that's still mutation. Alternati