`\nconst incrementCount = (counts: Record<string, number>, key: string): number => {\n  counts[key] = (counts[key] ?? 0) + 1;\n  return counts[key];\n};\n```\nThen countItemsByType: `counts[item.type] = incrementCount(counts, item.type);` Actually returns new count; we can just call `incrementCount(counts, item.type)`. But assignment still there. Or use return. In validateDamages, `const damageCount = incrementCount(damageTypeCounts, damage.itemType); if (damageCount > itemTypeCounts[damage.itemType]) throw`. This removes duplicated expression and improves intent. Mass: helper adds a function with params, one conditional, one assignment, plus invocations in callers. Let's calculate.\n\nAlternatively, we can implement `countBy` generic? Too much.\n\nAnother idea: `validateDamages` throws generic Error messages; tests only expect `.toThrow()`. Could be improved but not needed.\n\nPotential refactor with biggest impact: The `totalModifiedItemPremiumFor` formula is complex. We can improve variable names and add explaining variable. Current:\n```\nconst totalModifiedItemPremiumFor = (\n  items: Item[],\n  itemTypeCounts: Record<string, number>\n): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => {\n    const groupBase = basePremiumForItemGroup(item.type, itemTypeCounts[item.type]);\n    const share = groupBase / itemTypeCounts[item.type];\n    return sum + share * itemPremiumMultiplier(item);\n  }, 0);\n```\nNames `groupBase` and `share` are okay. Could rename `share` to `itemBaseShare`. But minor.\n\nMaybe there is duplication between `calculateQuotePremium` and `totalBasePremiumFor`: policyBase = totalBasePremiumFor(itemTypeCounts). Then totalModifiedItemPremiumFor recomputes groupBase per item. Not exactly duplicate.\n\nCould we remove `totalBasePremiumFor`? It is used only for policyBase in quote premium. It is also conceptually the basis of modifier. Keep.\n\nPotential refactor: group type interfaces together at top. This is a structural improvement (Rule 2). Let's do that.